the hogwarts castle model it's the best  model i've made this is the one that  stays in my mind harry potter and the  philosopher's stone has a set  combination of the massive warner  brothers studios and actual location  shoots not only did they utilize cgi  animation and tons of historical  landmarks but they also built their own  sets from the ground up  number one  also known as hogwarts the school of  witchcraft and wizardry this famous  castle that you can actually visit is  located in the english county of  northumberland they use this as a set  for the first and second films in the  harry potter franchise specific  locations in the castle like the outer  bailey the courtyards and the lions arch  were all very pertinent in the film  cinematography  number two  yes olmwit castle was used for a variety  of shots but the cool thing about harry  potter is that they used a combination  of practical sets cgi and animation to  give us the hogwarts we know and love a  team of 86 artists and crew members  created a mold of the hogwarts castle  that measures over 50 feet in diameter  and has over 2500 fiber optic lights  it was created for the sole purpose of  aerial photography and was digitally  scanned for the cgi scenes  number three the final challenge for  harry ron and hermione was a lot more  practical than you'd think every single  one of those chess pieces was sculptured  with fiberglass and steel by the art  department these chess pieces were so  large and had such presence they were  really very impressive to see  the king was over 10 feet tall the  pieces were remote controlled so they  did actually move across the board  even those explosions were real  number four  home of some great feasts  for the first day anyway the food in the  great hall was 100 real and the cast was  encouraged to eat the food  but unfortunately it did end up sitting  out and rotting for up to four days you  wound up smelling the great hall before  you even stepped inside  another thing the cast and crew  struggled with was the floating candles  they might look incredible but they  proved to be a bit of a dangerous hassle  not only did the crew have to light them  and lift them up on wires before each  scene  but they also had to have a fire  extinguisher on hand at all times daniel  radcliff recounts the experience and the  harry potter 20th anniversary returned  to hogwarts one of my favorite moments  on set ever was the moment when all the  floating candles started burning through  the ropes that tied them to the ceiling  and just started falling around the  great hall  number five  the iconic spot where harry learns he  can talk to snakes specifically the  reptile house in the london zoo located  in regents park london opened in 1927  the reptile house holds boaz cobras  crocodiles rattlesnakes chameleons and  iguanas instead of the python that harry  set free the real enclosure houses a  black mamba  number six  who wouldn't want to visit this place  leiden hall market is located in london  and it was used as exterior shots for  diagon alley most notably as the  entrance to the leaky cauldron though  its beautiful victorian exterior was  built in 1881 the market itself dates  all the way back to 1321. it was  originally a meat poultry and game  market but now it serves as a modern  shopping center full of boutiques  restaurants and cafes  this historical location which started  at the site of a manor has undergone an  immense amount of changes partially due  to what's known as the great fire but it  still remains a popular destination for  locals and tourists alike number 7.  king's cross holds a special place in jk  rowling's heart not only is that where  harry potter begins his journey in  wizardry  but it's also where her parents met  she says the station is evocative and  symbolic for her though the filmmakers  utilized the interior for scenes in  harry potter they actually used saint  patrick's station for the exterior shots  king's cross was built in 1852 they  found the terracotta design outside of  saint pancras to be a bit easier on the  eyes  number eight more specifically duke  humphreys library  this is part of the budlian library in  oxford and is only accessible through a  private tour this is home to the scene  where harry walks through the hogwarts  library with his invisibility cloak on  with the intention to steal a book from  the restricted stacks  number nine  bought out by warner brothers leaves in  studios home of all of the harry potter  movies spent millions on a harry potter  attraction site for fans to visit  because the books were still being  written during the production of harry  potter and the philosopher's stone they  made sure to keep every prop and set  piece in pristine condition so the fans  could see everything up close  leaves in studios is one of the largest  and most state-of-the-art filmmaking  facilities in the entire world home to  major films like the dark knight star  wars the hobbit and many many more  number 10  christ church holds a lot of very  important history not only in the harry  potter world though the historical  college was named by king henry viii and  was later used as a royal base during  the english civil war the christ church  great hall was the inspiration behind  the hogwarts dining hall  you'll notice the vast amount of  similarities between the two the bodily  staircase leading up to christ church  hall is used when hogwarts first years  walk up the stairs and are greeted by  professor mcgonagall  number eleven  the only practical thing about the  quidditch scenes were the broomsticks  everything else was achieved with a rig  and a green screen we'll program the rig  with our animation data so it looks like  the actor is steering the room and he's  riding around they would shoot each  character individually with the  appropriate lighting and camera angles  and then add the background and scenery  in post takes one two and three they  love it takes four and five  they're getting a little bit tired and  their bottoms probably a bit according  to the actors they did end up leaving  every time with a majorly sore butt  number 12.  yes it's an actual train properly known  as the jacobite steam express it's a  steam train located in glenfin and  viaduct scotland not only is it an  actual form of travel  but you can get on it and follow the  same journey that harry and all the  other hogwarts students did during the  film  number thirteen  not a place we were very fond of but an  actual suburban home nonetheless in the  later movies they ended up building a  set for four privet drive but before  they had the budget to do that they used  the house on 12 picket clothes in  bracknell the house was purchased a few  years ago and it's safe to say that the  owners definitely made a profit number  14 also known as hogsmeade station it  seems like there are quite a few iconic  train stations in the philosopher's  stone  this one holds two major scenes in the  film the first one is when the first  year students step off the hogwarts  express for the first time and the  second one when hagrid waves goodbye to  harry and the others at the end number  15.  also known as gringotts bank the only  bank in the wizarding world opened by  king george v in 1918 and built with  imported materials from australia hence  it's on the nose name this building is a  major landmark in real life  but for the potterheads it holds a  special place  harry potter and the philosopher's stone  is full of beautiful practical sets  historical landmarks churches and actual  suburban homes we've basically compiled  a list of places for every potter head  to visit what would be your first stop  you
 

